Verdict: MAJOR  
Retrieval adequacy: adequate

**Strengths**
- The revised issue body is materially tighter than the earlier draft: it now narrows to a foundation-only disclosure layer, separates `operator` vs `project` scope, requires row-level provenance, and explicitly defers normalization, ingestion, and downstream consumers.
- The current code surface supports that decomposition cleanly. Existing cost code is sanction-point oriented in [src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/calibration_schema.py](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/blob/main/src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/calibration_schema.py), [public_dataset.py](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/blob/main/src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/public_dataset.py), and [cost_predictor.py](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/blob/main/src/worldenergydata/cost/calibration/cost_predictor.py), so a separate disclosure layer is the right boundary.

**Findings by severity**

Critical
- None.

High
- The canonical plan artifact is still internally inconsistent with the revised issue and is not approval-ready as written. [docs/plans/2026-04-21-issue-334-annual-operator-disclosures-dataset.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/worldenergydata/blob/main/docs/plans/2026-04-21-issue-334-annual-operator-disclosures-dataset.md) still proposes package-root API expansion via `src/worldenergydata/cost/__init__.py`, while the revised issue explicitly says no package-root API expansion.
- The same plan artifact still describes an over-broad schema and helper surface that violates the new bounded scope. Its pseudocode includes generic `metric_value`, helper query APIs, and non-monetary enum members like counts/dates, which conflicts with the revised “typed annual as-reported monetary disclosure schema” and “foundation-only” claims.
- Linkage is still underspecified at the testable-contract level. The revised issue says exact `(operator, project_name)` linkage to existing `CostDataPoint` for project-scope rows only, but the plan/test surface does not yet require a linkage field or a test that operator-scope rows are never linked. That leaves the main acceptance claim partially rhetorical instead of executable.

Medium
- Provenance acceptance and planned tests are misaligned. The revised issue says every seed record must have `source_title`, `source_url`, `page_reference`, `quoted_text`, and `confidence`, but the current plan test language still allows weaker “page reference or quote support” style validation.
- The revised issue says the seed should be tiny and curated (`<=12` in your request summary), but the plan artifact does not encode an explicit dataset-size cap. Without that, “minimal” can drift during implementation.
- Restatement handling is named as an open risk but not constrained in the data contract. For annual disclosures, a record model usually needs at least one explicit convention for restated vs originally reported values, even if broader reconciliation is deferred. Otherwise duplicate year/project/operator rows can become ambiguous immediately.

Low
- The revised issue says no downstream integration, but the current plan still frames exports more broadly than necessary. Exporting from `cost.data_collection` is consistent; touching package root is not.
- The existing issue body and current plan both use slightly inconsistent field naming around `metric_name_reported` vs `as_reported_metric_name`. That is minor, but it will create avoidable churn in tests and docs.

**Missing tests**
- A schema test proving only monetary disclosure fields are accepted in v1 and that generic `metric_value` is absent.
- A test proving `scope="operator"` rows cannot carry or use `CostDataPoint` linkage fields.
- A test proving `scope="project"` rows use only exact `(operator, project_name)` linkage and do not attempt fuzzy matching.
- A test asserting every seed row has all required provenance fields populated, not just some citation support.
- A test enforcing the curated seed cap.
- A test for duplicate/restated same-year rows so the dataset cannot silently admit ambiguous records.

**Scope creep concerns**
- The main scope-creep vector is the stale plan artifact, not the revised issue body.
- Specifically: package-root exports, helper query APIs, and non-monetary metric vocabulary would all expand this from a T2 foundation issue into a broader modeling/API issue.

**Weakest assumption**
- That exact `(operator, project_name)` matching is sufficient even for a tiny seed without first defining how project naming variants and phase naming are represented in the dataset itself.

**Most likely implementation failure mode**
- The implementer follows the stale plan doc instead of the tightened issue body and ships a broader schema/API surface than the revised approval target allows.

**Most likely test gap**
- Tests pass on loader/schema happy paths but never assert the negative boundaries: no package-root export, no operator-row linkage, no generic metric field, no missing provenance fields.

**Future issues suggested**
- Add one small follow-up or acceptance addendum specifically for restatement identity rules if that is not folded into #337.
- Keep #335 separate, but require #334 to define the exact linkage field shape now, even if broader linkage hardening stays deferred.

**Review confidence**
- High. The revised issue body is close to approval shape, but the canonical plan artifact still reflects the older, broader design and leaves a real approval blocker until those mismatches are removed.
